'''Donald John Trump''' (born June 14, 1946) is an American politician, media personality, and businessman who is the 47th [[president of the United States]].<!--DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence (including linking) without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#Current consensus]], item 70.--> A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he served as the 45th president from 2017 to 2021.<!--DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence (including linking) without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#Current consensus]], item 70.-->

Born in New York City, Trump graduated from the [[University of Pennsylvania]] in 1968 with a [[bachelor's degree]] in economics. He became the president of his family's real estate business in 1971, renamed it [[the Trump Organization]], and began acquiring and building skyscrapers, hotels, casinos, and golf courses. After six business bankruptcies in the 1990s and 2000s, he began side ventures. From 2004 to 2015, he hosted the reality television show ''[[The Apprentice (American TV series)|The Apprentice]]''. A political outsider, Trump won the [[2016 United States presidential election|2016 presidential election]] against Democratic nominee [[Hillary Clinton]].

In [[First presidency of Donald Trump|his first term]], Trump imposed [[Executive Order 13769|a travel ban]] on citizens from six Muslim-majority countries, expanded the [[U.S.–Mexico border wall]], and implemented [[Trump administration family separation policy|a family separation policy]].<!-- This sentence must contain a summary of Trump's actions on immigration, including the Muslim travel ban, the wall, and the family separation policy; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#Current consensus]], item 52. --> He rolled back environmental and business regulations, signed the [[Tax Cuts and Jobs Act]] of 2017, and appointed [[Donald Trump Supreme Court candidates|three Supreme Court justices]]. In foreign policy, Trump withdrew the U.S. from agreements on climate, trade, and Iran's nuclear program, began [[China–United States trade war|a trade war with China]], and met with North Korean leader [[Kim Jong Un]] without reaching an agreement on denuclearization.<!-- DO NOT REMOVE preceding sentence without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#Current consensus]], item 44. --> In response to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]], he downplayed its severity, contradicted health officials, and signed the [[CARES Act]] stimulus. Trump was impeached in 2019 for abuse of power and obstruction of Congress, and in 2021 for incitement of insurrection; the Senate acquitted him in both cases. After his first term, [[Historical rankings of presidents of the United States|scholars and historians ranked]] him as one of the worst presidents in American history.<!--DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#Current consensus]], item 54.-->

Trump is the central figure of [[Trumpism]].<!--DO NOT EXPAND on Trumpism without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#Current consensus]], item 68.--> Many of his comments and actions have been characterized as racially charged, racist or misogynistic,<!--DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus, see [[Talk:Donald Trump#Current consensus]], items 30 and 51.--> and he has made false and misleading statements and promoted conspiracy theories to a degree unprecedented in American politics.<!--DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#Current consensus]], items 49 and 53.--> After losing the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential election]] to [[Joe Biden]], Trump [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|attempted to overturn the outcome]], culminating in the [[January 6 Capitol attack]] in 2021. In 2023, Trump was held liable in civil cases [[E. Jean Carroll v. Donald J. Trump|for sexual abuse and defamation]] and [[New York business fraud lawsuit against the Trump Organization|for business fraud]]. In 2024, he was found guilty [[Prosecution of Donald Trump in New York|of falsifying business records]], making him the first U.S. president convicted of a felony.  After winning the [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 presidential election]] against [[Kamala Harris]], Trump was sentenced to a penalty-free [[Discharge (sentence)|discharge]], and [[Indictments against Donald Trump|two other felony indictments]] against him were dismissed.

Trump began his second term by [[Pardon of January 6 United States Capitol attack defendants|pardoning around 1,500 January&nbsp;6 rioters]], imposing sweeping tariffs against major trading partners, creating the [[Department of Government Efficiency]] presidential task force, and initiating [[2025 United States federal mass layoffs|mass layoffs of the federal workforce]]. Trump's broad and extensive use of [[executive order]]s has drawn numerous lawsuits challenging their legality.

